PATTERNS = [
{"id":"navy","t":"A coast guard or navy arrives within weeks","cases":["rose-island","rem-island","minerva","minerva-1982-and-fiji-tonga","operation-atlantis","xlii-seastead-phuket","vemerana","republic-of-indian-stream","liberland","free-republic-of-verdis","kingdom-of-sedang","wrangel-island-expedition"],"body":["The people who actually build something near a coast mostly find out how fast a state moves. The Dutch boarded REM Island off Noordwijk in 1964, five days after passing a law for that purpose. Italy seized Rose Island eight weeks after it declared independence in 1968 and later demolished it. Tonga's king removed the Republic of Minerva within months in 1972, after Australia, New Zealand, Fiji and others met and agreed that Tonga should annex the reef. When Americans returned to Minerva in 1982, Tongan troops forced them off in three weeks.","The same sequence runs on today. The Thai navy raided a one-cabin seastead 14 nautical miles off Phuket in April 2019, and the couple who lived there, having already left, faced a police complaint under a sovereignty law that carries life in prison or death. The sources found do not report how that case ended. Croatian police arrested Liberland's founder in September 2023 after supporters built shelters on a Danube floodplain, eight years after first detaining visitors there.","In practice: a claim near a coast rarely reaches a court argument. An unarmed group has no way to answer a boat, and so the speed of the response tells you more than the legal theory. Founders tend to read the law of the sea carefully and plan less for what to do when a patrol boat arrives."]},
{"id":"lines","t":"States move the line after someone tests it","cases":["sealand","radio-caroline","knock-john-radio-essex","radio-390-red-sands","rem-island","ss-rex-gambling-ships","abalonia-cortes-bank","grand-capri-atlantis-isle-of-gold","minerva"],"body":["Many founders aim for a gap in a legal boundary, and the boundary then changes. Radio Caroline broadcast from international waters off Suffolk from 1964. Britain could not reach the ship, so it made it a crime for its own people to supply or advertise on such stations in 1967. REM Island sat beyond Dutch waters, so the Dutch claimed the seabed under it. The US courts treated reefs off Florida as part of its continental shelf in the Grand Capri litigation, and that reasoning later answered proposals for Cortes Bank.","Roy Bates moved to Roughs Tower after losing the argument at Knock John, which turned out to lie inside the 3-mile limit. Roughs Tower was outside it. Britain's 1987 extension to 12 miles then took in the water around Sealand. Sealand's story is usually told as a case of a gap held open, and it is also a case of a state choosing not to act on a gap it had already closed.","In practice: a legal gap describes today's law and it stays open only for as long as nobody important is bothered. New legislation can reach you through your suppliers, customers, nationals or the ground under you."]},
{"id":"paper","t":"Selling the country before there is one","cases":["new-utopia","dominion-of-melchizedek","kingdom-of-enenkio","lunar-embassy","lunar-registry","lindsay-coles-early-deeds","asgardia","wirtland","akon-city","bitcoin-city","praxis","life-at-sea","blueseed"],"body":["The cheapest part of a new state is its paperwork, and so some attempts become businesses in paper. New Utopia offered $350 million in bonds on a website for concrete platforms over a Caribbean bank; the US Securities and Exchange Commission found it had raised about $24,000 when it froze the assets in 1999. Melchizedek, which held no land, sold passports at $10,000 and bank licences at $50,000. Enenkio claimed Wake Island and sold bonds until a US judge froze those too. Dennis Hope's lunar deeds, $19.99 to $22.49 an acre, brought in $9 million according to Discover, on a claim he says the United Nations never answered.","Not every paper country is a fraud. Asgardia's citizens, some 290,000 by its own count in 2019, knew they were joining a project, and Hope's buyers mostly treat a deed as a novelty. The Life at Sea cruise, by contrast, collapsed in 2023 because the company had no ship to sail.","In practice: buying citizenship or land from a new state buys you whatever its seller delivers and nothing a state will honour. Securities law follows the seller to the buyer's country whatever flag sits on the website, and the buyers usually pay."]},
{"id":"money","t":"Money is the easy part","cases":["sealand","minerva","operation-atlantis","new-atlantis","rose-island","hutt-river","principality-of-seborga","westarctica","flandrensis","liberland","republic-of-molossia","republic-of-uzupis","asgardia","celestia","somaliland","transnistria"],"body":["Nearly every attempt issues money, usually before it issues anything else. Sealand struck coins pegged to the US dollar from 1972. Minerva had a 35-dollar coin. Operation Atlantis struck the deca, a decagram of silver, on a soap press. Hemingway's raft of New Atlantis named a currency called the scruple, payable in fish hooks and shark teeth. Today the same step is a token: Liberland's Merit, Asgardia's planned Solar.","Using a coin among friends is simple. Paying someone outside is where it stops. No correspondent bank will open an account for an unrecognised issuer, currency codes under ISO 4217 follow recognised country codes, and any token sold to outsiders meets the FATF travel rule and the US and EU stablecoin laws. Transnistria runs a real central bank, and its ruble can be exchanged only inside the territory, even with a real army and 30 years behind it.","In practice: internal currency is the most achievable part of the plan in almost every location on this map. It tells you little about whether the place will work, since residents still need dollars, euros or bitcoin for what they import."]},
{"id":"sea","t":"The sea and the budget end more attempts than states do","cases":["abalonia-cortes-bank","new-atlantis","aisland-landi","operation-atlantis","ms-satoshi","freedom-ship","villa-vie-odyssey","blueseed","oceania-atlantis-project","autopia-saya-de-malha","mars-one","aurora-station","bigelow-aerospace","planetary-resources-dsi","voyager-station"],"body":["The ship meant to found Abalonia on Cortes Bank in 1966 was wrecked by waves before any government had responded. Storms took Hemingway's raft in 1966 and a storm destroyed Stiefel's first boat in the Bahamas. In February 2024 a wave broke Samuele Landi's barge in two off Dubai, and he and two employees died. The Satoshi cruise ship never hit weather at all: in 2020 no insurer would give liability cover, so there could be no crew and no flag.","Off Earth the failure is financial. Mars One went bankrupt in a Basel court in 2019. Orion Span's Aurora Station closed without a launch. Bigelow laid off all its staff in 2020. Freedom Ship's price climbed to about $16 billion and stayed on paper. Villa Vie's residential cruise ship was laid up in October 2026 with its residents put ashore in Cambodia.","In practice: engineering and capital are the real gatekeepers for the places law leaves open. The less a state cares about a location, usually the harder it is to survive there."]},
{"id":"flag","t":"A flag buys peace, and costs the independence","cases":["ms-the-world","women-on-waves","radio-caroline","iss-intergovernmental-agreement","vast-haven-1","nasa-commercial-stations","asgardia","flags-of-convenience-space","fcc-starlink-licensing","swarm-spacebees-fcc","mircorp","xlii-seastead-phuket"],"body":["The long-running communities outside land borders have something in common: they kept a state in the arrangement. The World, an apartment ship, flies the Bahamas flag, and has done since 2002. Women on Waves carries Dutch law onto the high seas under a Dutch flag, and won at the European Court of Human Rights when Portugal blocked it with warships in 2004. On the International Space Station each module stays under the state that registered it.","The rule follows to orbit. Asgardia-1 reached space on a US launch. Swarm launched four satellites from India in 2018 after the US regulator refused them a licence, and it paid $900,000 because it was an American firm using radio ground stations in Georgia. Registration, launch licences, radio spectrum and insurance all run through states.","In practice: a person can already live outside any single country's daily reach, by paying a flag state for the privilege. Whether this counts as what founders wanted depends on what they wanted. Rules on board and private money work. A place of your own in law does not come with it."]},
{"id":"forgotten","t":"Paper claims last because nobody needs to stop them","cases":["westarctica","flandrensis","talossa-antarctic-claim","bir-tawil-kingdom-of-north-sudan","bir-tawil-later-claimants","morac-songhrati-meads","celestia","lunar-embassy","gajardo-moon-chile","republic-of-molossia","aerican-empire","kingdom-of-redonda","elgaland-vargaland","republic-of-kugelmugel","ladonia","conch-republic"],"body":["The longest-lived claims in the unclaimed places are usually the ones with no people on the ground. Westarctica has claimed Marie Byrd Land since 2001, and its founder wrote to many governments and heard back only from other micronations. Flandrensis claims five Antarctic islands from Belgium, and now registers as a Belgian non-profit about melting ice. Jeremiah Heaton's flag on Bir Tawil in 2014 led to a UN observer application that went nowhere. James Mangan recorded a claim to all of outer space with a Cook County clerk in 1949, and it was never contested.","Micronations inside countries work the same way. Molossia, on a family's land in Nevada, pays its property tax. Their founders usually know the score and use the form for art, protest, tourism or a hobby.","In practice: the survival of these claims says nothing about their legal strength. Nobody tests them because nothing is at stake, and the moment a claim costs a state money or attention, it joins a different pattern on this list."]},
{"id":"host","t":"What one government grants, the next can take back","cases":["prospera-zede","difc","forest-city","akon-city","bitcoin-city","network-state-school","floating-island-french-polynesia","oceanix-busan","seapods-panama","vatican-lateran-treaty","mount-athos","sark","freetown-christiania"],"body":["If you want real courts and your own tax rules today, the working route runs through a government that agrees to give them to you. Dubai created the DIFC by its own laws in 2004, with common-law courts in English running since 2006. Honduras amended its constitution for ZEDEs in 2013, and Próspera built on Roatán. In 2022 a new president repealed the law and in September 2024 the Supreme Court struck down the system. The company has an investment-treaty claim that started at $10.775 billion.","Promises fall away in the same way. French Polynesia signed a memorandum with the Seasteading Institute in 2017, and after local opposition the government said in 2018 that it had no legal force. Sapco, Senegal's tourism agency, ended Akon City in 2025. Malaysia's city council revoked the Network School's licence in July 2026, and within hours Kazakhstan's digital ministry signed a memorandum to host a campus. The durable examples, Mount Athos and the Vatican, rest on treaties or constitutions long settled.","In practice: the deal with a host is a contract with whoever holds office. It can give you more self-rule than any seastead has had, for as long as voters and courts go along with it, and the risk sits in local opposition more than in foreign navies."]},
{"id":"force","t":"Recognition has followed force and long standing","cases":["somaliland","taiwan","transnistria","northern-cyprus","republic-of-artsakh","rojava","wa-state","order-of-malta","vatican-lateran-treaty","zapatista-territories"],"body":["The governments on this map that any UN member treats as a state did not start with a declaration on empty ground. Somaliland has held elections and run its own currency since 1991, and Israel became the first UN member to recognise it on 26 December 2025. Taiwan, with about 23.3 million people, keeps ties with 11 UN members and the Holy See. Northern Cyprus has Turkey alone, because Security Council Resolution 541 called on others to refuse.","Force can also undo all of it. Artsakh's government of 32 years ended in the Azerbaijani offensive of 2023. Rojava's administration joined the Syrian state in August 2026, after a government offensive that began that January. The exception with no land is the Order of Malta, which lost its last territory to Napoleon in 1798 and has kept diplomatic relations with over 100 states on the strength of centuries of standing.","In practice: recognition is a political decision made by existing states about their own interests. Nothing in this catalogue suggests a new group can earn it by meeting a checklist, and none of the attempts on open sea, ice or in space has been recognised by a UN member."]},
{"id":"states","t":"States keep the open places for themselves","cases":["bogota-declaration","artemis-accords","ilrs","moon-agreement","us-space-act-2015","luxembourg-space-resources-2017","uae-japan-space-resources","villa-las-estrellas","esperanza-base-births","antarctic-claims-article-iv","arktika-2007-flag","international-seabed-authority","metals-company","tongasat","adiz-high-seas"],"body":["While private claims fail, states go on shaping the same places with far more effect. Eight equatorial countries claimed geostationary orbit in Bogotá in 1976. Tonga filed for 16 orbital slots from 1990 and rented them out. Russia planted a titanium flag on the seabed at the North Pole in 2007. Chile and Argentina placed families in Antarctica, including the 1978 birth of Emilio Palma, on land where Art. IV of the treaty says such acts add nothing to a claim.","Today the contest is over resources. The United States passed a law in 2015 letting its citizens own what they mine in space, followed by Luxembourg, the UAE and Japan. The Artemis Accords, with 78 signatories as of October 2026, treat safety zones around activities as permitted, while China and Russia plan a rival lunar station. On the deep seabed, The Metals Company's US arm applied for permits under US law in 2025, outside the International Seabed Authority, which has opened an inquiry.","In practice: the treaties that bar sovereignty in these places bind states only partly, and practice is filling the gaps. A private founder is arriving in places where the rule for the next century is being written by others."]},
{"id":"gaps","t":"The old gaps closed when the neighbours decided","cases":["neutral-moresnet","republic-of-cospaia","couto-misto","free-state-of-bottleneck","no-mans-land-cimarron-territory","kowloon-walled-city","republic-of-indian-stream","clipperton-arbitration","island-of-palmas","kingdom-of-enclava"],"body":["Real gaps between countries have existed, some for a long time. Cospaia began with a 1440 drafting error over two creeks both called the Rio and stayed independent for nearly four centuries, until Tuscany and the Papal States divided it in 1826. Moresnet was left neutral in 1816 because the Netherlands and Prussia could not agree on a zinc mine, and lasted until the First World War and the Treaty of Versailles. Couto Misto ended by treaty in the 1860s. Kowloon's Walled City survived a 1948 eviction attempt and lasted until Britain and China agreed to clear it in the 1990s.","In each case people lived there because the neighbours found it cheaper to leave things as they were. International rulings since then tightened the idea of empty land: the Island of Palmas award of 1928 asks for continuous, peaceful display of state authority, and the Clipperton award of 1931 gave an empty island to the state that had claimed it properly.","In practice: today's remaining gaps, such as Bir Tawil and Gornja Siga, exist for the same reason. The neighbours' border arguments leave them unclaimed, and this offers a founder no standing, since states read empty land as open only to other states."]},
]

PATTERNS += [
{"id":"secession","t":"The route that has actually produced new states","cases":["south-sudan-2011","eritrea-1993","timor-leste-1999-2002","montenegro-2006","kosovo-2008","independence-referendum-record","biafra-1967","catalonia-2017","scotland-2014","bougainville-2019","new-caledonia-kanaky-referendums","compacts-of-free-association","abkhazia","south-ossetia","sahrawi-republic","bantustans-transkei-bophuthatswana"],"body":["New states do still appear, on land where a people already lives and usually after long conflict or a negotiated deal. South Sudan voted 98.83% for secession in January 2011, in a referendum set by a 2005 peace agreement with the government in Khartoum. Kosovo's assembly declared independence in 2008, and UN membership has stayed out of reach since then because Serbia objects. Of about 123 independence votes counted for this map, 59 ended in a No, 31 in a Yes and a recognised state, 16 in a Yes with no independence, and 12 in control on the ground without general recognition.","The other side of the record shows how much depends on the old state. Biafra declared independence in 1967, was recognised by five states, and lost a civil war. Western Australia voted Yes in 1933 and stayed in Australia. South Africa declared four Bantustans independent from 1976, and no other country accepted them, which shows recognition failing in the opposite direction.","In practice: this door opens for peoples with territory and history. A group of investors or settlers has no way through it, however careful its constitution."]},
{"id":"purchase","t":"Nobody sells a country to a private buyer","cases":["state-to-state-purchases","greenland-purchase-offers","kiribati-natoavatu-fiji","minerva","operation-atlantis","romanov-empire-bakov","bakov-arca-noe","hutt-river"],"body":["Sovereignty has been sold many times, between states. The United States paid $15 million for Louisiana in 1803, $7.2 million for Alaska in 1867 and $25 million for the Danish West Indies in 1916. When it offered Denmark $100 million in gold for Greenland in 1946, Denmark refused, and Danish and Greenlandic leaders refused again in 2019 and 2026.","Private founders have looked for the same deal and not found it. Michael Oliver tried to buy an island before piling sand on the Minerva Reefs. Werner Stiefel tried to buy the Prickly Pear Cays for $100,000 and the sale fell through. Kiribati, itself a state, paid US$8.6 million for land in Fiji in 2014, and that bought an estate under Fijian law.","In practice: buying land gets you a title deed from the government whose law continues to apply. A private island is the same thing with more water around it."]},
{"id":"coups","t":"Hiring soldiers to take a country","cases":["denard-comoros","seychelles-1981-coup","wonga-coup-equatorial-guinea","operation-red-dog-dominica"],"body":["A few people skipped the empty places and went for existing governments. In 1981 Mike Hoare led 53 mercenaries to the Seychelles disguised as rugby players and a beer-drinking club, and airport staff found a rifle in a bag. That same year white supremacists were arrested over Operation Red Dog, a plan to overthrow Dominica's government. In March 2004 police in Harare impounded a Boeing 727 with 64 former soldiers bound for Equatorial Guinea and arrested Simon Mann by the runway.","Bob Denard is the exception. He overthrew the Comoros government in 1978 with 43 men and ran its presidential guard for 11 years, until France removed him. He had French backing at the start, which set him apart from the others.","In practice: the plots failed at airports, boats and bank accounts, well before any fighting that mattered. Those that came near success depended on a government's quiet support."]},
{"id":"cracks","t":"People can live in the cracks, without governing them","cases":["nasseri-charles-de-gaulle","snowden-sheremetyevo","assange-ecuador-embassy","cyprus-buffer-zone-pyla","korean-dmz-villages","chernobyl-samosely","slab-city","kowloon-walled-city"],"body":["Some places fall between authorities in everyday life. Mehran Karimi Nasseri lived in Terminal 1 at Charles de Gaulle from 1988 to 2006. Julian Assange spent almost seven years in a set of rooms at Ecuador's London embassy, until Ecuador withdrew his asylum in 2019. About 10,000 people live and farm inside Cyprus's UN buffer zone. Chernobyl's self-settlers returned without permission, and an official census counted 314 of them in 2007.","None of these people held the ground. A host could end each arrangement, as Ecuador did, and peacekeepers or the military kept the rules in the buffer zones.","In practice: a gap can shelter a person for years. It gives them no power to make laws, admit others or deal with the outside world, which is what a founder is after."]},
{"id":"newground","t":"Up, down or newly risen, the law gets there first","cases":["graham-island-ferdinandea","surtsey-paris-match","hunga-tonga-new-island","nishinoshima-growth","south-talpatti-new-moore","cyberbunker","pionen-bahnhof","vivos-shelters","ad-coelum-downward","unclos-shelf-subsoil","a23a-iceberg","stratobus","sky-cruise"],"body":["Founders sometimes hope a new kind of place will escape the old rules. New ground does not: in 1831 a Sicilian official planted an oar on a volcanic islet within days of its appearing, and Britain's navy claimed it two weeks later, before it sank. Land rising from a country's seabed falls to that country.","Going down does not help either. The rule that a state's authority runs beneath its surface has a long history, and the operators of a former NATO bunker in the Netherlands, who called it the Republic of CyberBunker, faced Dutch prosecutors and then a German police raid on their second bunker in 2019. Icebergs are unsettled in law, but they melt. High platforms such as Stratobus remain uncrewed designs under the air law of the country below.","In practice: no new layer of the planet has yet offered more than a brief or unsettled gap, and the people using one have usually depended on a country for their supplies and their licences."]},
{"id":"prior","t":"Peoples who were already there","cases":["haudenosaunee-passport","mcgirt-v-oklahoma","nunavut","hawaiian-sovereignty-movement","ganienkeh","murrawarri-republic","sovereign-yidindji-government","central-tibetan-administration","republic-of-lakotah","zapatista-territories","cheran"],"body":["The biggest gains in self-rule on this list went to peoples with a claim older than the state. The Inuit negotiated Nunavut, 2,093,190 square kilometres, which became a Canadian territory in 1999. In McGirt v. Oklahoma in 2020 the US Supreme Court held that the Muscogee reservation had never been ended, and most of eastern Oklahoma was later treated the same way for crimes involving Native Americans. Cherán in Mexico won the right to choose its government by custom.","Full separation stays out of reach. The Haudenosaunee have issued passports since at least 1923; Japan and Ireland have accepted them, while Britain refused them for a lacrosse team in 2010. Murrawarri and Yidindji declarations in Australia have not changed Australian law.","In practice: where these claims succeed, they succeed through courts and negotiation with the existing state. They grow self-rule inside it, a very different thing from founding a place outside it."]},
]
